// Irresistible: Joe Messinger's deer-hair drake of the 1930s, in the Adams Irresistible dressing: a clipped deer-hair body under Catskill wings and hackle.

id irresistible
name "Irresistible"
aliases "Adams Irresistible" "Deer Hair Drake"
type dry
imitates mayfly attractor
naturals mayfly-dun
difficulty advanced
species trout
season june july august september
origin originator="Joe Messinger Sr." region="Morgantown, West Virginia, USA"
description "A moose hair tail, a body of grey deer hair spun and clipped to a cone, upright grizzly hackle-tip wings and a brown and grizzly hackle."
history """
    Joe Messinger Sr. of Morgantown, West Virginia, tied it in the 1930s as his Deer Hair Drake,
    after an egg-laden female drake. Dick Talleur tells that the outdoor writer Ken Lockwood,
    given one by Messinger, said it looked irresistible. Messinger's own was two-tone, blue-grey
    deer hair over white with a claret hackle, and according to the Southern Fly Fishing Hall of
    Fame its bunches were knotted on rather than spun. The Adams Irresistible tied today, with
    the Adams's wings and hackle, is credited to Messinger by Trident.
    """
fishing """
    A high floater for fast, broken water: riffles, runs and the heads of pools. The packed
    deer hair keeps it up where a feather-bodied dry would drown, and it suggests many insects
    while staying easy to see. Fish it on a dead drift.
    """
sources {
    "Rocky River TU: Irresistible (Tom Adams and Alen Baker)" url="http://www.rockyrivertu.org/irresistible.html"
    "Southern Fly Fishing Hall of Fame: Joe M. Messinger Sr." url="https://southernflyfishinghalloffame.com/hof-inductee/joe-m-messinger-sr/"
    "Weirton Daily Times: Man carries on father's fly-tying legacy (John McCoy, AP)" url="https://www.weirtondailytimes.com/news/local-news/2020/01/man-carries-on-fathers-fly-tying-legacy/"
    "Fly Anglers Online: Irresistible, Just Old Flies #119 (Thomas C. Duncan Sr., quoting Dick Talleur, archived)" url="https://web.archive.org/web/2015/http://www.flyanglersonline.com/features/oldflies/part119.php"
    "Fly Fishing the Sierra: Adams Irresistible" url="https://flyfishingthesierra.com/?p=20459"
    "Trident Fly Fishing: How to tie the Adams Irresistible (Stephen D'Angelo)" url="https://www.tridentflyfishing.com/blogs/all/how-to-tie-the-adams-irresistible-fly-pattern"
    "Fish4Flies: Irresistible Adams" url="https://www.fish4flies.com/Dry/Hackled/Irresistible-Adams"
}
// Photos for checking proportions while authoring (pnpm refcheck); never published.
references {
    "https://www.fish4flies.com/img/flies/Original/T4656Irrisistible%20Adams.jpg" page="https://www.fish4flies.com/Dry/Hackled/Irresistible-Adams" license="All rights reserved; reference only" note="Side view; the cone body clear"
    "https://flyfishingthesierra.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/12/irresist.jpg" page="https://flyfishingthesierra.com/?p=20459" license="All rights reserved; reference only" note="Side view in the vise"
    "http://www.rockyrivertu.org/uploads/2/2/6/4/22648242/published/54-light-irresistable0197.jpg?1633523904" page="http://www.rockyrivertu.org/irresistible.html" license="All rights reserved; reference only" note="Side view in the vise, a light body"
}

sits hackle
hook dry-standard size=12
thread black size="8/0"

materials {
    tail moose-mane label="Moose body hair"
    body deer-hair color=grey label="Natural grey deer body hair"
    wings hackle-tips color=grizzly label="Grizzly hen hackle tips"
    hackle-brown cock-hackle color=brown fibre=1.5
    hackle-grizzly cock-hackle color=grizzly fibre=1.5
}

steps {
    attach-thread at=0.85 note="Two eye-lengths behind the eye."
    wrap-thread to=0.0
    // Sources: slightly shorter than to a shank; the photos show 1.1 to 1.7 gapes.
    tie-in tail length=1.5 spread=20 note="Six to eight moose hairs, stacked; soft turns first so they don't flare."
    trim tail
    spin body to=0.58 note="Three or four pencil-thick bunches, underfur combed out, each pushed back hard."
    // Read from the photos: about 0.4 gape across at the tail and 0.7 at the front.
    trim body leave=0.35 shape=cone note="Square cuts top, bottom and sides, then angled back to the tail; square off the front as a seat for the wings. Keep the tail clear."
    wrap-thread to=0.76
    // Trident: about a shank long.
    tie-in wings length=1.9 angle=90 spread=22 note="Concave sides out, stood up and divided to about 45°."
    wrap-thread to=0.6
    tie-in hackle-brown note="At the front of the body."
    tie-in hackle-grizzly
    wrap-thread to=0.92
    wind hackle-brown to=0.9 turns=5 note="Three turns behind the wings, two or three in front."
    tie-off hackle-brown
    wind hackle-grizzly to=0.9 turns=5 note="Through the brown, the same way."
    tie-off hackle-grizzly
    whip-finish
}
